 While estradiol itself has not been used clinically by intravenous injection, certain estrogen preparations such as conjugated estrogens and estramustine phosphate are available in formulations indicated for intravenous injection. [ 366 ]  Both of these medications act in part as prodrugs of estradiol. [ 367 ]   [ 368 ]   [ 369 ]  The intravenous formulation of conjugated estrogens is available at a dose of 25   mg per injection and is used in the treatment of abnormal uterine bleeding due to its ability to rapidly and temporarily enhance coagulation . [ 366 ]  It has also been used off-label to treat severe bleeding after hysteroscopic metroplasty and as an emergency contraceptive . [ 366 ]   [ 370 ]   [ 368 ]  The formulation is given in a single injection but can be repeated after 6 to 12   hours if necessary.
